  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15}=\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/845121f81ff0dbe29a073319fbdeeab86e1c8b145471e8d3ec655d49d05d86cd.png)",
